[AS7's Figure 13.33 (ideal transformer, symbolic)]

t,2,3,1,n
r2,3,4,z2
e2,4,0,vs2

analysis: dc
note: In this problem, we describe an ideal transformer (the t element) for the first time. Find VTh and ZEq to the right of nodes a and b, entirely symbolically. The transformer refers the source by the turns ratio and the impedance by its square, which is what an ideal one does. Everything here is a name.
image: https://learn.symbulator.com/assets/circuit/as7f1333.png
tool: th
n1: 2
n2: 0
rounding: exact
si: no
units: yes


[AS7's Example 13.8 (ideal transformer, opposite dots)]

e,1,0,120
r1,1,2,4-6j
t,2,3,1,-2
ro,3,0,20

analysis: ac
note: Find the source current I1, the output voltage Vo, and the complex power supplied by the source (the Evaluate line, -se). One turn count is negative because the dots are of opposite polarity; which of the two you negate makes no difference.
